    "abstract": "Kenya has in recent years experienced a surge in extreme rainfall events, which continue to pose serious risks to human life, infrastructure, and the wider economy. This study aimed to develop a non-stationary Generalized Pareto Distribution(GPD). Its performance was compared with the classical stationary Peaks-Over-Threshold (POT) model to improve the modeling and prediction of extreme rainfall events. Accurately modeling these extremes is therefore essential to support sound risk assessment and informed decision-making. An Extreme Value Theory(EVT)\u2013based threshold exceedance framework was used to model rainfall extremes, contrasting stationary and non-stationary Generalized Pareto formulations. Under the non-stationary framework, key environmental covariates were incorporated. These included Sea Surface Temperature (SST), Atmospheric Moisture Content (AMC), and Impervious Surface Percentage (ISP). These covariates account for ocean-driven climate variability, atmospheric moisture availability, and land-surface modifications influencing local atmospheric moisture, respectively. The findings indicate that the most suitable model was one in which the shape parameter varied with SST and the scale parameter with AMC. This model yielded lower information criterion values, reflecting a superior fit. This preference was further supported by a likelihood-ratio test, which indicated significance at p &amp;lt; 0.05. The model enabled the estimation of return levels and the assessment of rainfall-related risks in Kenya. By accounting for climatic and land-surface drivers, the study offers a stronger basis for risk assessment and delivers guidance for urban planning, policymaking, and strategies for disaster preparedness.",
    "field": "Statistical modeling of climate extremes",
    "problem": "Classical stationary models for extreme rainfall underestimate risk by assuming constant parameters, failing to incorporate the influence of changing environmental covariates, such as sea surface temperature, which are critical in a non-stationary climate.",
    "method": "This study develops a non-stationary Generalized Pareto Distribution model where scale and shape parameters vary with Sea Surface Temperature and Atmospheric Moisture Content to improve the modeling and prediction of extreme rainfall events in Kenya.",
    "task": "The task is to improve the modeling of extreme rainfall events in Kenya by developing a non-stationary Generalized Pareto Distribution model incorporating environmental covariates and comparing its performance against a classical stationary Peaks-Over-Threshold model.",
